WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.950
(bell ringing)

00:00:01.950 --> 00:00:03.927
- [Joanna] You may have heard of ChatGPT.

00:00:03.927 --> 00:00:05.910
- The stagnance of these two characters

00:00:05.910 --> 00:00:08.280
is what propels each
respective plot forward

00:00:08.280 --> 00:00:09.810
and changes those around them.

00:00:09.810 --> 00:00:12.210
- [Joanna] A new artificially
intelligent chatbot

00:00:12.210 --> 00:00:15.750
that you can command to
write pretty much any text.

00:00:15.750 --> 00:00:18.060
- Existentialism exists
as a refusal of the notion

00:00:18.060 --> 00:00:20.220
of our lives having some
predetermined reasoning.

00:00:20.220 --> 00:00:21.390
- [Joanna] You also may have heard

00:00:21.390 --> 00:00:25.470
that ChatGPT is so good and
writes so much like a human

00:00:25.470 --> 00:00:27.270
that it can be used to write essays

00:00:27.270 --> 00:00:28.950
and do homework assignments.

00:00:28.950 --> 00:00:31.230
- An existential text with its themes

00:00:31.230 --> 00:00:35.173
of individuality, freedom and
the search of meaning of life.

00:00:35.173 --> 00:00:36.006
(bell ringing)

00:00:36.006 --> 00:00:38.970
- So yes, I pulled a Billy
Madison and went back

00:00:38.970 --> 00:00:43.970
to AP literature to see how far
AI and just AI could get me.

00:00:44.250 --> 00:00:47.160
I'm a new student totally in high school.

00:00:47.160 --> 00:00:48.510
I enrolled in 12th grade

00:00:48.510 --> 00:00:51.750
at High Tech High School
in Secaucus, New Jersey.

00:00:51.750 --> 00:00:52.583
Okay, fine.

00:00:52.583 --> 00:00:55.170
The teacher, Michael
Diamond, let me in his class

00:00:55.170 --> 00:00:57.990
since he'd already been
playing around with the AI tool

00:00:57.990 --> 00:01:00.960
and was intrigued by
my cheating experiment.

00:01:00.960 --> 00:01:01.890
- I have a quick question.

00:01:01.890 --> 00:01:04.530
- Yes.
- Is the Wi-Fi down?

00:01:04.530 --> 00:01:05.760
- The Wi-Fi is down.

00:01:05.760 --> 00:01:07.291
- Great, thanks.

00:01:07.291 --> 00:01:08.124
(students chuckling)

00:01:08.124 --> 00:01:08.957
- Sorry.

00:01:08.957 --> 00:01:11.060
- So did ChatGPT pass or fail?

00:01:11.060 --> 00:01:11.910
(buzzer beeping)

00:01:11.910 --> 00:01:15.030
We devised two challenges to find out.

00:01:15.030 --> 00:01:17.730
Challenge number one, write an essay.

00:01:17.730 --> 00:01:18.930
Before coming to class,

00:01:18.930 --> 00:01:21.390
Mr. Diamond emailed me my assignment.

00:01:21.390 --> 00:01:23.220
- [Michael] In a 500 to 1,000-word essay,

00:01:23.220 --> 00:01:24.090
compose an argument

00:01:24.090 --> 00:01:26.220
that attempts to situate
"Ferris Bueller's Day Off"

00:01:26.220 --> 00:01:28.200
as an existentialist text.

00:01:28.200 --> 00:01:29.370
- [Joanna] The assignment also said

00:01:29.370 --> 00:01:31.020
to make explicit comparisons

00:01:31.020 --> 00:01:33.450
or connections between
"Ferris Bueller's Day Off"

00:01:33.450 --> 00:01:36.060
and "The Metamorphosis" by Franz Kafka.

00:01:36.060 --> 00:01:38.070
I edited the assignment text slightly,

00:01:38.070 --> 00:01:41.010
pasted it into ChatGPT
and in about a minute,

00:01:41.010 --> 00:01:43.770
I had it, a full essay.

00:01:43.770 --> 00:01:45.300
I was impressed.

00:01:45.300 --> 00:01:47.550
It drew parallels to
Gregor, the main character,

00:01:47.550 --> 00:01:50.040
in Kafka's book, and to Ferris.

00:01:50.040 --> 00:01:51.600
It had varied sentence structure

00:01:51.600 --> 00:01:54.330
and good grammar and
was decently organized.

00:01:54.330 --> 00:01:56.040
I put it in a document and submitted it

00:01:56.040 --> 00:01:58.050
to Mr. Diamond via email.

00:01:58.050 --> 00:01:59.688
Then he did his thing.

00:01:59.688 --> 00:02:02.271
(upbeat music)

00:02:05.240 --> 00:02:06.870
(bell ringing)

00:02:06.870 --> 00:02:11.340
- You ended up with a three
out of six on the AP rubric.

00:02:11.340 --> 00:02:14.280
I was a little generous
because it's early in the year.

00:02:14.280 --> 00:02:15.300
You showed some promise

00:02:15.300 --> 00:02:17.370
but there are some issues
you really need to work on.

00:02:17.370 --> 00:02:19.260
You got your thesis point right away.

00:02:19.260 --> 00:02:20.730
You wrote "Ferris Bueller's Day Off"

00:02:20.730 --> 00:02:22.200
is a film that can be interpreted

00:02:22.200 --> 00:02:25.530
as an existentialist text with
its themes of individuality,

00:02:25.530 --> 00:02:27.750
freedom and the search
for meaning in life.

00:02:27.750 --> 00:02:30.090
Unfortunately, in that
same opening paragraph,

00:02:30.090 --> 00:02:34.830
you misattributed a line that
was said by Cameron to Ferris.

00:02:34.830 --> 00:02:35.700
- You sure of that?

00:02:35.700 --> 00:02:36.690
- That I am sure of.

00:02:36.690 --> 00:02:37.770
- I am not gonna sit

00:02:37.770 --> 00:02:41.880
on my ass the events that affect me unfold

00:02:41.880 --> 00:02:43.350
to determine the course of my life.

00:02:43.350 --> 00:02:44.790
I'm gonna take a stand.

00:02:44.790 --> 00:02:46.530
- [Joanna] There were
other factual inaccuracies

00:02:46.530 --> 00:02:47.430
in my essay.

00:02:47.430 --> 00:02:49.800
ChatGPT confused Cameron with Ferris,

00:02:49.800 --> 00:02:51.330
saying Ferris was reserved

00:02:51.330 --> 00:02:53.490
and rarely seen next to his peers.

00:02:53.490 --> 00:02:55.680
- I think maybe you were
thinking of Cameron,

00:02:55.680 --> 00:02:57.030
but you wrote Ferris.

00:02:57.030 --> 00:02:59.880
- Yeah, I think that's probably
what I was thinking. Yeah.

00:02:59.880 --> 00:03:01.260
- You said that the setting

00:03:01.260 --> 00:03:04.020
of "The Metamorphosis" was
suburbia, which it was not.

00:03:04.020 --> 00:03:05.400
It was an urban setting.

00:03:05.400 --> 00:03:08.460
No, we need to work on being
more accurate in your details

00:03:08.460 --> 00:03:10.920
and I did not see a lot
of vivid writing here.

00:03:10.920 --> 00:03:13.230
- [Joanna] Not easy for
a professional journalist

00:03:13.230 --> 00:03:14.670
and writer to hear.

00:03:14.670 --> 00:03:16.530
- The overall quality
of your writing puts you

00:03:16.530 --> 00:03:18.630
in the lower 30th percentile I think.

00:03:18.630 --> 00:03:21.270
I think you may have the mind to get there

00:03:21.270 --> 00:03:23.670
but it's the skills that
you need to work on.

00:03:23.670 --> 00:03:24.930
- That doesn't feel good.

00:03:24.930 --> 00:03:27.810
Challenge one, a pass,
because technically,

00:03:27.810 --> 00:03:31.470
a three out of six on an AP
essay is like a B or a C.

00:03:31.470 --> 00:03:34.230
I should note that plagiarism
detectors like this one

00:03:34.230 --> 00:03:37.140
from Grammarly are not
likely to flag AI writing

00:03:37.140 --> 00:03:40.548
as plagiarism since it is original text.

00:03:40.548 --> 00:03:42.720
Do you think you would've
had any idea this was written

00:03:42.720 --> 00:03:44.220
by a computer?

00:03:44.220 --> 00:03:46.350
- I don't think that I
would catch it to be honest.

00:03:46.350 --> 00:03:47.580
I mean, first of all, if you look

00:03:47.580 --> 00:03:50.490
at the the actual context
of when I'm grading, right?

00:03:50.490 --> 00:03:51.810
I'm going through 60 of these

00:03:51.810 --> 00:03:55.410
at a clip and I'm looking
for specific things.

00:03:55.410 --> 00:03:58.110
- [Joanna] Luckily for Mr.
Diamond, companies are working

00:03:58.110 --> 00:04:00.840
on ways to detect AI writing.

00:04:00.840 --> 00:04:02.160
Challenge number two,

00:04:02.160 --> 00:04:04.500
participate in a group discussion.

00:04:04.500 --> 00:04:05.550
- You're gonna get in your core groups

00:04:05.550 --> 00:04:08.250
and you're going to go
through the next three steps

00:04:08.250 --> 00:04:11.370
and you're gonna first, review the story.

00:04:11.370 --> 00:04:13.590
Each group has been
assigned one in advance.

00:04:13.590 --> 00:04:16.837
- My group was assigned to read
Denis Johnson's short story,

00:04:16.837 --> 00:04:18.660
"Car Crash While Hitchhiking"

00:04:18.660 --> 00:04:20.640
from the collection "Jesus' Son."

00:04:20.640 --> 00:04:22.140
My group mates immediately got

00:04:22.140 --> 00:04:25.440
to the first discussion
question about the characters.

00:04:25.440 --> 00:04:27.810
- The cop did actually stop
him to make sure he actually

00:04:27.810 --> 00:04:29.790
was a part of the crime scene.

00:04:29.790 --> 00:04:32.100
- Could you also say maybe the doctor?

00:04:32.100 --> 00:04:32.933
Or is that?

00:04:32.933 --> 00:04:34.680
- Yeah, I definitely
think the doctor because.

00:04:34.680 --> 00:04:36.150
- When I searched ChatGPT

00:04:36.150 --> 00:04:38.970
for information about the
main character in the story,

00:04:38.970 --> 00:04:41.640
the bot said it didn't have any at all.

00:04:41.640 --> 00:04:43.950
So I changed the prompt
and asked it questions

00:04:43.950 --> 00:04:46.590
about the collection's
title, "Jesus' Son,"

00:04:46.590 --> 00:04:49.260
which kind of got me somewhere.

00:04:49.260 --> 00:04:51.480
Is there a character named Billy?

00:04:51.480 --> 00:04:53.790
- Billy? Not in this chapter.

00:04:53.790 --> 00:04:55.530
- Does anyone in this
chapter have anything

00:04:55.530 --> 00:04:56.906
to do with addiction?

00:04:56.906 --> 00:04:57.739
- Yes.
- Oh yes.

00:04:57.739 --> 00:04:59.250
- Yes. Okay.

00:04:59.250 --> 00:05:02.119
And does he attempt to navigate
the complexities of life?

00:05:02.119 --> 00:05:03.597
- Yeah.
- Yeah, yeah, he did.

00:05:03.597 --> 00:05:04.740
- Oh, okay.

00:05:04.740 --> 00:05:06.900
But that was really
all I could contribute.

00:05:06.900 --> 00:05:08.010
I tried different prompts

00:05:08.010 --> 00:05:10.500
like what's the bigger
philosophical question,

00:05:10.500 --> 00:05:11.940
but I got no help.

00:05:11.940 --> 00:05:13.953
Not going well at all.

00:05:13.953 --> 00:05:16.800
While OpenAI, the company behind ChatGPT,

00:05:16.800 --> 00:05:18.780
trained this tool on a massive trove

00:05:18.780 --> 00:05:21.930
of data gathered from the
internet and other sources,

00:05:21.930 --> 00:05:23.970
it clearly hasn't read this book.

00:05:23.970 --> 00:05:26.670
Finally, I just tried to skim the text

00:05:26.670 --> 00:05:29.460
but ultimately, I just
asked a very nice human

00:05:29.460 --> 00:05:30.660
to summarize it for me.

00:05:30.660 --> 00:05:33.180
- So basically this guy
has been like traveling,

00:05:33.180 --> 00:05:35.190
traveling through, he's
been like hitchhiking

00:05:35.190 --> 00:05:36.780
through like the woods and everything.

00:05:36.780 --> 00:05:38.100
- Challenge two.
(buzzer buzzing)

00:05:38.100 --> 00:05:39.420
Fail.

00:05:39.420 --> 00:05:41.670
Of course, the whole
purpose of this experiment

00:05:41.670 --> 00:05:44.820
wasn't really to try to get an A in AP Lit

00:05:44.820 --> 00:05:47.880
but to see the capabilities
and limits of AI

00:05:47.880 --> 00:05:49.410
as an educational tool.

00:05:49.410 --> 00:05:52.623
So some have started
to call this CheatGPT.

00:05:53.700 --> 00:05:55.200
Are you worried about that?

00:05:55.200 --> 00:05:58.230
- I am worried about the easiness, right?

00:05:58.230 --> 00:05:59.400
That's what cheating really is

00:05:59.400 --> 00:06:01.650
is like the easiest way to do a thing.

00:06:01.650 --> 00:06:03.840
They're not learning what I'm asking them

00:06:03.840 --> 00:06:05.850
if the internet can give them a full essay

00:06:05.850 --> 00:06:08.340
because they're missing out
on the process of writing.

00:06:08.340 --> 00:06:11.100
And I'm more concerned
with the the possibilities

00:06:11.100 --> 00:06:14.310
to prevent learning and beget compliance

00:06:14.310 --> 00:06:16.680
so they can get pushed
through school, so to speak.

00:06:16.680 --> 00:06:18.210
- [Joanna] My biggest takeaway returning

00:06:18.210 --> 00:06:19.410
to high school 20 years

00:06:19.410 --> 00:06:22.980
after graduating, how
ingrained tech already is.

00:06:22.980 --> 00:06:25.320
Students already lean
on Google and Grammarly

00:06:25.320 --> 00:06:28.050
and more to help them
with their assignments.

00:06:28.050 --> 00:06:30.180
It's nuts to think artificial intelligence

00:06:30.180 --> 00:06:32.850
won't be the next tech
to change education.

00:06:32.850 --> 00:06:35.550
- I am definitely gonna try
to figure out how to use it.

00:06:35.550 --> 00:06:38.400
I feel like with all tech,
it's easy to be scared of it.

00:06:38.400 --> 00:06:39.840
And the good news is, at least here,

00:06:39.840 --> 00:06:41.460
there's some teachers that
I've been talking with

00:06:41.460 --> 00:06:44.760
that are like excited
about getting ahead of it

00:06:44.760 --> 00:06:47.070
and incorporating it
and not running from it

00:06:47.070 --> 00:06:49.341
because I don't think
we're gonna outrun it.

00:06:49.341 --> 00:06:51.180
(playful music)

00:06:51.180 --> 00:06:52.950
- Hi guys.
- Hi.

00:06:52.950 --> 00:06:54.550
- Can you be my friend?
- Sure.

